### What do the Object methods beyond equals/hashCode do?

Every Java class inherits 11 methods from Object. Beyond equals/hashCode/toString, four important groups exist.

### What does getClass() do?

```java
Account ref = new SavingsAccount("SAV001", java.math.BigDecimal.ZERO);

Class<?> cls = ref.getClass();
cls.getName();        // "com.bank.SavingsAccount" (actual type, not variable type)
cls.getSimpleName();  // "SavingsAccount"
cls.getSuperclass();  // Account.class

// Exact type check (no subclasses):
if (ref.getClass() == SavingsAccount.class) { /* exact match only */ }
// vs instanceof (includes subclasses):
if (ref instanceof Account) { /* SavingsAccount passes too */ }

// getClass() is final: cannot be overridden.
```

### What does clone() do?

```java
public class Trade implements Cloneable {
    @Override
    public Trade clone() {
        try {
            return (Trade) super.clone(); // shallow copy
        } catch (CloneNotSupportedException e) {
            throw new AssertionError(); // never happens: we implement Cloneable
        }
    }
}
// clone() is protected on Object: must override as public.
// Without Cloneable: CloneNotSupportedException.
// Discouraged: prefer copy constructors or records.
```

### What do wait/notify/notifyAll do?

```java
public class TradeQueue {
    private final java.util.Queue<Trade> queue = new java.util.LinkedList<>();
    private static final int MAX = 10;

    public synchronized void produce(Trade trade) throws InterruptedException {
        while (queue.size() == MAX) {
            wait(); // queue full: release lock, suspend this thread
        }
        queue.add(trade);
        notifyAll(); // wake up all waiting consumers
    }

    public synchronized Trade consume() throws InterruptedException {
        while (queue.isEmpty()) {
            wait(); // queue empty: release lock, suspend this thread
        }
        Trade trade = queue.poll();
        notifyAll(); // wake up all waiting producers
        return trade;
    }
}

// Why synchronized is required:
// wait() releases the object's monitor lock. If you do not hold the lock, there is nothing to release.
// Without synchronized: IllegalMonitorStateException at runtime.
```

### Why is finalize() deprecated?

```java
// OLD (deprecated since Java 9):
@Override
protected void finalize() throws Throwable {
    closeConnection(); // unreliable: no guarantee when or if GC calls this
}

// NEW: use AutoCloseable + try-with-resources
public class DbConnection implements AutoCloseable {
    @Override
    public void close() {
        connection.close(); // deterministic cleanup
    }
}
try (var conn = new DbConnection()) {
    // use conn
} // close() called automatically, always
```

### How do all Object methods compare?

| Method | Purpose | Can override? | Notes |
|--------|---------|--------------|-------|
| equals() | Value equality | Yes | Override with hashCode |
| hashCode() | Hash code for collections | Yes | Must be consistent with equals |
| toString() | String representation | Yes | Used by println, debugger |
| getClass() | Runtime type info | No (final) | Returns Class<?> |
| clone() | Shallow copy | Yes | Requires Cloneable |
| wait() | Thread waits for notify | No (final) | Must be in synchronized |
| notify() | Wakes one waiting thread | No (final) | Must be in synchronized |
| notifyAll() | Wakes all waiting threads | No (final) | Must be in synchronized |
| finalize() | GC cleanup | Yes | Deprecated since Java 9 |

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is calling wait() or notify() outside a synchronized block.

```java
// WRONG: not in synchronized
// trade.wait(); // IllegalMonitorStateException

// CORRECT: inside synchronized
synchronized(trade) { trade.wait(); }
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Modern concurrency uses java.util.concurrent (BlockingQueue, CountDownLatch, CompletableFuture) instead of raw wait/notify.
- finalize() has been replaced by Cleaner (Java 9) and PhantomReference for GC-triggered cleanup.
- getClass() is used in correct equals() implementations to prevent symmetry violations in inheritance hierarchies.
